.DS_Store

# Android/IntelliJ
#
build/
.idea
.gradle
local.properties
*.iml
*.hprof
.cxx/

# generated inline modules
app/src/main/java/inline/

# Bundle artifacts
*.jsbundle

# Release signing secrets — never commit a real keystore or its passwords
# (ARCHITECTURE.md §8). keystore.properties.example is the committed
# template; the real file (and the keystore it points to) stay local to
# whoever runs the release build.
keystore.properties
*.keystore
*.jks

# The debug keystore is the opposite case: it's what "*.keystore" above
# would otherwise exclude, and it IS committed — every clone (including CI)
# needs the same file or `:app:validateSigningDebug` fails on a missing one.
#
# Only the storePassword/keyPassword/keyAlias ("android"/"android"/
# "androiddebugkey" — see https://developer.android.com/studio/publish/app-signing#debug-mode)
# are the public, standard convention; those are not a secret and were
# never the issue. The actual key PAIR inside this file must stay unique to
# this project rather than being Android/React Native's literal shared
# template keystore (which this file used to be, byte for byte — same
# fingerprint as whatever countless other tutorial/boilerplate repos on
# GitHub also ship). Google Play Protect's on-device reputation check
# flagged exactly that: a certificate it has seen attached to thousands of
# unrelated apps, on an app that also asks for SMS permissions, got a hard
# "blocked to protect your device" with no install-anyway option. Generated
# fresh for this project with `keytool -genkeypair` (same alias/passwords,
# a new random key) to get out from under that shared-certificate history.
# Replacing it again means every device with VOXO already installed has to
# uninstall before the next build can go on — Android won't update an app
# in place across a different signing certificate.
!app/debug.keystore

# Firebase config for FCM — identifies one specific Firebase project, so
# each deployment supplies its own (see PUSH_SETUP.md). Gradle reads it
# from here; the CI build copies it in from the GOOGLE_SERVICES_JSON
# repository secret.
app/google-services.json

# Sentry source-map upload config. Holds auth.token, which grants READ
# access to the org — never commit it. CI writes this from secrets; see
# SENTRY_SETUP.md.
/sentry.properties
